d ${y}_{2}=4.$ \n\nTherefore, $y=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}].$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186479088 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186478993 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186479088 #edb/ref 1069 "q-107169" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186479088 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186479088 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the matrix $L$ and the vector $b,$ given by \n\n$$\nL=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 7 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,b=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ -9\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nIf $A=LU$ is the LU factorization of some $2\\times 2$ matrix $A,$ find the vector $y$ such that \n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}Ly=b \\\\ Ux=y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nis equivalent to $Ax=b.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186479088 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "501022bc-dca6-55f9-9b78-5c4a339dd1a1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186479088 #edb/ref 1074 "First, notice that since $A=LU,$ we can write\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Ax=b\\,⟹\\,{{(LU)}_{⏟}}_{A}x=b.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, setting $Ux=y,$ we obtain\n\n$$\nL{{(Ux)}_{⏟}}_{y}=b\\,⟹\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}Ly=b \\\\ Ux=y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now solve $Ly=b.$ Writing this equation in matrix form, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 7 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} \\\\ {y}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ -9\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives the system of equations\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1}=-2 \\\\ 7{y}_{1}+{y}_{2}=-9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe solution to this system is ${y}_{1}=-2$ and ${y}_{2}=5.$ \n\nTherefore, $y=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}].$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186479090 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186479021 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186479090 #edb/ref 1069 "q-32579" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186479090 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186479090 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the matrix $L$ and the vector $b,$ given by \n\n$$\nL=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,b=[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nIf $A=LU$ is the LU factorization of some $3\\times 3$ matrix $A,$ find the vector $y$ such that\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}Ly=b \\\\ Ux=y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nis equivalent to $Ax=b.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186479090 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "53971d39-ffaa-5c20-9081-d54743d61b0a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186479090 #edb/ref 1074 "First, notice that since $A=LU,$ we can write\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Ax=b\\,⟹\\,{{(LU)}_{⏟}}_{A}x=b.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, setting $Ux=y,$ we obtain\n\n$$\nL{{(Ux)}_{⏟}}_{y}=b\\,⟹\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}Ly=b \\\\ Ux=y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now solve $Ly=b.$ Writing this equation in matrix form, we get\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} \\\\ {y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{3}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThis gives the system of equations\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1}=6 \\\\ -{y}_{1}+{y}_{2}=-2 \\\\ {y}_{1}-{y}_{2}+{y}_{3}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can compute the solution to this system using forward substitution, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} & =6, \\\\ {y}_{2} & =-2+{y}_{1} \\\\  & =-2+6 \\\\  & =4, \\\\ {y}_{3} & =-{y}_{1}+{y}_{2} \\\\  & =-(6)+4 \\\\  & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n$y=[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 4 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}].$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186479095 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186478984 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186479095 #edb/ref 1069 "q-107184" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186479095 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186479095 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the matrix $L$ and the vector $b,$ given by \n\n$$\nL=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -4 & -3 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,b=[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ -5 \\\\ 9\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nIf $A=LU$ is the LU factorization of some $3\\times 3$ matrix $A,$ find the vector $y$ such that\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}Ly=b \\\\ Ux=y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nis equivalent to $Ax=b.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186479095 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "7099a838-c9d8-54d6-9fec-92d9fefe3598" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186479095 #edb/ref 1074 "First, notice that since $A=LU,$ we can write\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Ax=b\\,⟹\\,{{(LU)}_{⏟}}_{A}x=b.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, setting $Ux=y,$ we obtain\n\n$$\nL{{(Ux)}_{⏟}}_{y}=b\\,⟹\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}Ly=b \\\\ Ux=y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now solve $Ly=b.$ Writing this equation in matrix form, we get\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -4 & -3 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} \\\\ {y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{3}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ -5 \\\\ 9\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThis gives the system of equations\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1}=-4 \\\\ 2{y}_{1}+{y}_{2}=-5 \\\\ -4{y}_{1}-3{y}_{2}+{y}_{3}=9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can compute the solution to this system using forward substitution, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} & =-4, \\\\ {y}_{2} & =-5-2{y}_{1} \\\\  & =-5-2(-4) \\\\  & =3, \\\\ {y}_{3} & =9+4{y}_{1}+3{y}_{2} \\\\  & =9+4(-4)+3(3) \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n$y=[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186479106 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186478972 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186479106 #edb/ref 1069 "q-105758" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186479106 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186479106 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the matrix $L$ and the vector $b,$ given by \n\n$$\nL=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -8 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,b=[\\begin{aligned}8 \\\\ -14\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nIf $A=LU$ is the LU factorization of some $2\\times 2$ matrix $A,$ find the vector $y$ such that \n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}Ly=b \\\\ Ux=y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nis equivalent to $Ax=b.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186479106 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "88def0e2-16e4-59a7-96bb-d850c4ea1c49" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186479106 #edb/ref 1074 "First, notice that since $A=LU,$ we can write\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Ax=b\\,⟹\\,{{(LU)}_{⏟}}_{A}x=b.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, setting $Ux=y,$ we obtain\n\n$$\nL{{(Ux)}_{⏟}}_{y}=b\\,⟹\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}Ly=b \\\\ Ux=y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now solve $Ly=b.$ Writing this equation in matrix form, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -8 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} \\\\ {y}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}8 \\\\ -14\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives the system of equations\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1}=8 \\\\ -8{y}_{1}+{y}_{2}=-14.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe solution to this system is ${y}_{1}=8$ and ${y}_{2}=50.$ \n\nTherefore, $y=[\\begin{aligned}8 \\\\ 50\\end{aligned}].$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186479110 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186478995 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186479110 #edb/ref 1069 "q-107497" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186479110 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186479110 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the LU decomposition of the matrix $A$ and the vector $b,$ given by\n\n$$\nA={{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 9 & 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{L}{{[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 6\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{U},\\,b=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nIf $⟨{x}_{1},{x}_{2}⟩$ and $⟨{y}_{1},{y}_{2}⟩$ are the solutions to the systems $Ax=b$ and $Ly=b$ respectively, what is the value of ${x}_{2}\\cdot {y}_{1}?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186479110 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "9ad4c6c3-50cf-5ba9-ae9f-35f5ce660c90" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186479110 #edb/ref 1074 "First, notice that since $A=LU,$ we can write\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Ax=b\\,⟹\\,{{(LU)}_{⏟}}_{A}x=b.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, setting $Ux=y,$ we obtain\n\n$$\nL{{(Ux)}_{⏟}}_{y}=b\\,⟹\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}Ly=b \\\\ Ux=y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the two systems in turn.\n\n- First, we solve $Ly=b.$ Writing this equation in matrix form, we get\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 9 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} \\\\ {y}_{2}\\end{aligned}]=b=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThis gives the system of equations\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1}=1 \\\\ 9{y}_{1}+{y}_{2}=6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe solution to this system is ${y}_{1}=1$ and ${y}_{2}=-3.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\ny=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n- Next, we solve $Ux=y.$ Writing this equation in matrix form, we get\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 6\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]=y=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThis gives the system of equations\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}-{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}=1 \\\\ 6{x}_{2}=-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe solution to this system is ${x}_{2}=-\\frac{1}{2}$ and ${x}_{1}=-2.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\nx=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ -\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, ${x}_{2}\\cdot {y}_{1}=(-\\frac{1}{2})\\cdot 1=-\\frac{1}{2}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186479113 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186479040 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186479113 #edb/ref 1069 "q-32566" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186479113 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186479113 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the LU decomposition of the matrix $A$ and the vector $b,$ given by\n\n$$\nA={{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{L}{{[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{U},\\,b=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nIf $⟨{x}_{1},{x}_{2}⟩$ and $⟨{y}_{1},{y}_{2}⟩$ are the solutions to the systems $Ax=b$ and $Ly=b$ respectively, what is the value of $\\sqrt{{x}_{1}^{2}+{y}_{2}^{2}}?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186479113 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "ab64b08c-fec6-5dff-b1cc-ff3d1f5e73c7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186479113 #edb/ref 1074 "First, notice that since $A=LU,$ we can write\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Ax=b\\,⟹\\,{{(LU)}_{⏟}}_{A}x=b.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, setting $Ux=y,$ we obtain\n\n$$\nL{{(Ux)}_{⏟}}_{y}=b\\,⟹\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}Ly=b \\\\ Ux=y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the two systems in turn.\n\n- First, we solve $Ly=b.$ Writing this equation in matrix form, we get\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} \\\\ {y}_{2}\\end{aligned}]=b=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThis gives the system of equations\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1}=3 \\\\ 3{y}_{1}+{y}_{2}=-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe solution to this system is ${y}_{1}=3$ and ${y}_{2}=-12.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\ny=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -12\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n- Next, we solve $Ux=y.$ Writing this equation in matrix form, we get\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]=y=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -12\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThis gives the system of equations\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}-3{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=3 \\\\ {x}_{2}=-12.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe solution to this system is ${x}_{2}=-12$ and ${x}_{1}=-5.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\nx=[\\begin{aligned}-5 \\\\ -12\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{{x}_{1}^{2}+{y}_{2}^{2}} & =\\sqrt{(-5)^{2}+(-12)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{25+144} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{169} \\\\  & =13.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186479139 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186479048 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186479139 #edb/ref 1069 "q-105932" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186479139 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186479139 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the matrix $L$ and the vector $b,$ given by \n\n$$\nL=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -2 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,b=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nIf $A=LU$ is the LU factorization of some $3\\times 3$ matrix $A,$ find the vector $y$ such that\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}Ly=b \\\\ Ux=y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nis equivalent to $Ax=b.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186479139 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "e717653a